WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.164 --> 00:00:07.039
It's gonna rain.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.392
Well, how'd you figure
that out, killer?

3
00:00:10.544 --> 00:00:12.503
How come you changed your vote?

4
00:00:12.504 --> 00:00:15.006
Well, it just seemed to me
there was room for doubt.

5
00:00:15.007 --> 00:00:17.300
You haven't got a leg to stand on.
You know that, I hope.

6
00:00:17.301 --> 00:00:20.386
I don't feel that way. There were a
lot of details that never came out.

7
00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:24.015
- Yeah. Well, good luck.
- Oh, come on. You're like everybody else.

8
00:00:24.016 --> 00:00:26.325
You think too much.
You get mixed up.

9
00:00:27.478 --> 00:00:28.610
You know what I mean?

10
00:00:28.636 --> 00:00:31.522
Now, listen. I don't think
you have any right...

11
00:00:31.523 --> 00:00:34.192
Loudmouth.

12
00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:29.430
Look at that come down, will you?

13
00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:32.458
You know...

14
00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:35.044
this reminds me of a
storm we had last...

15
00:01:35.045 --> 00:01:38.589
Oh, when was that? November...

16
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.759
I don't know. Something. It was
quite a storm we had there.

17
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.220
It was right in the
middle of a game.

18
00:01:44.221 --> 00:01:46.931
Uh, we were behind 7-6...

19
00:01:46.932 --> 00:01:49.600
but we just started to move the
ball, right off the tackle.

20
00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:52.478
Started cutting right...
slash here and slash right in...

21
00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:54.355
cut right through.

22
00:01:54.356 --> 00:01:56.941
And, uh...

23
00:01:56.942 --> 00:01:59.251
We had this kid Slattery, you know?

24
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.781
A real... He was an ox.
You know, a real ox.

25
00:02:03.782 --> 00:02:06.367
I wish I had another
one just like him.

26
00:02:06.368 --> 00:02:10.955
I forgot to tell you. I'm the assistant head
coach at the Andrew J. McCorkle High School.

27
00:02:10.956 --> 00:02:13.124
- Oh, yeah?
- That's out in Queens.

28
00:02:13.125 --> 00:02:15.960
And, uh, anyway...

29
00:02:15.961 --> 00:02:18.129
we're starting to
move along real nice.

30
00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.399
Their whole line is just...
they're just coming apart.

31
00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:26.429
And it starts to come down
cats and dogs, just like this.

32
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:29.156
Just whoosh...
you know, right down.

33
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.811
Well, that was murder, you know?

34
00:02:33.812 --> 00:02:36.480
I swear, I... I nearly bawled.

35
00:02:36.481 --> 00:02:38.916
You just...
You couldn't go nowhere.

36
00:02:40.068 --> 00:02:41.502
Well.

37
00:02:57.044 --> 00:02:59.645
Hey, what is it with this fan here?
How come...

38
00:03:07.304 --> 00:03:09.138
Hey! Huh?

39
00:03:09.139 --> 00:03:12.533
Must have been on the
same switch with the lights.

40
00:03:13.435 --> 00:03:16.704
Well, things are
looking up here, huh?

41
00:03:17.272 --> 00:03:18.873
Yeah, huh?

42
00:03:23.612 --> 00:03:25.713
Boy. That's better.

43
00:03:32.287 --> 00:03:33.871
Hey!

44
00:03:33.872 --> 00:03:35.639
Two points.

45
00:03:39.544 --> 00:03:41.645
Any of you guys ever
go to the Garden?

46
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.801
That's a damn stupid thing to do.

47
00:03:46.802 --> 00:03:49.070
Oh, I'm sorry. I, uh...

48
00:03:50.013 --> 00:03:51.489
You know?

49
00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.700
Pardon me.

50
00:03:55.686 --> 00:03:58.104
Well, how do you like it?
Even Steven.

51
00:03:58.105 --> 00:04:00.564
Pretty surprising, isn't it?

52
00:04:00.565 --> 00:04:02.166
Yes.

53
00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.446
Say, that business before, when
that tall guy... what's-his-name...

54
00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:10.241
was trying to bait me...
That doesn't prove anything.

55
00:04:10.242 --> 00:04:12.910
I'm a pretty excitable person.

56
00:04:12.911 --> 00:04:16.831
I mean, where does he come off calling me
a public avenger, sadist and everything?

57
00:04:16.832 --> 00:04:20.418
Anyone in his right mind
would blow his stack, huh?

58
00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:22.795
He was just trying to bait me.

59
00:04:22.796 --> 00:04:24.897
He did an excellent job.

60
00:04:27.592 --> 00:04:29.885
I'll tell you what I think.
We're going nowhere here.

61
00:04:29.886 --> 00:04:32.513
I'm ready to walk into court right
now and declare a hung jury.

62
00:04:32.514 --> 00:04:34.432
There's no point in this
thing going on any more.

63
00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:35.850
Yeah. I go for that too.

64
00:04:35.851 --> 00:04:38.769
Listen, let's take it in to the judge and let
the kid take his chances with 12 other guys.

65
00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.689
I don't think the judge will accept a hung
jury. We haven't been in here very long.

66
00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.441
Well, let's find out.

67
00:04:43.442 --> 00:04:45.234
I'm not in favor of that.

68
00:04:45.235 --> 00:04:48.529
Listen. This kid wouldn't stand a chance
with another jury, and you know it.

69
00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.449
Come on! We're hung. Nobody's gonna
change his vote. Let's take it inside.

70
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.743
You still don't think there's
room for reasonable doubt?

71
00:04:53.744 --> 00:04:55.119
No, I don't.

72
00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.495
Pardon.

73
00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:59.832
Maybe you don't fully understand
the term "reasonable doubt."

74
00:04:59.833 --> 00:05:02.476
What do you mean
I don't understand?

75
00:05:03.462 --> 00:05:05.504
Boy, how do ya like this guy?

76
00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.256
I'm tellin' ya, they're all alike.

77
00:05:07.257 --> 00:05:10.176
They come over here, running for their life,
and before they can take a deep breath...

78
00:05:10.177 --> 00:05:12.636
they're telling us how
to run the show, huh?

79
00:05:12.637 --> 00:05:14.513
Boy, the arrogance of this guy.

80
00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:19.185
Hey, all right. Let's stop the
arguing for about two minutes in here.

81
00:05:19.186 --> 00:05:21.645
Now, who's got something
constructive to say?

82
00:05:21.646 --> 00:05:25.274
I'd like to go over something,
if you gentlemen don't mind.

83
00:05:25.275 --> 00:05:28.027
An important point for the
prosecution was the fact...

84
00:05:28.028 --> 00:05:30.863
that after the boy claimed
he'd been at the movies...

85
00:05:30.864 --> 00:05:32.948
during the hours that the
killing took place...

86
00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:36.911
couldn't remember the names of the movies
or the stars who appeared in them.

87
00:05:36.912 --> 00:05:39.497
This gentleman here has put up
that point in here several times.

88
00:05:39.498 --> 00:05:41.040
That's correct.

89
00:05:41.041 --> 00:05:43.000
It was the only alibi
the boy offered...

90
00:05:43.001 --> 00:05:45.586
and he himself couldn't back
it up with any details at all.

91
00:05:45.587 --> 00:05:48.923
Putting yourself in the boy's place,
do you think you could remember details...

92
00:05:48.924 --> 00:05:52.468
after an upsetting experience such as
being slapped in the face by your father?

93
00:05:52.469 --> 00:05:55.763
I think so, if there were any
special details to remember.

94
00:05:55.764 --> 00:05:57.468
The boy couldn't remember
the names of the movies

95
00:05:57.469 --> 00:05:59.016
he saw because be wasn't
there that night.

96
00:05:59.017 --> 00:06:01.143
According to the police
testimony in court...

97
00:06:01.144 --> 00:06:04.355
the boy was questioned by the detectives
in the kitchen of his apartment...

98
00:06:04.356 --> 00:06:07.358
while the body of his father was
lying on the floor in the bedroom.

99
00:06:07.359 --> 00:06:10.027
Do you think you could remember
details under those circumstances?

100
00:06:10.028 --> 00:06:12.113
- I do.
- Under great emotional stress?

101
00:06:12.114 --> 00:06:14.698
- Under great emotional stress.
- He remembered them correctly in court.

102
00:06:14.699 --> 00:06:16.742
He named the pictures and the
stars who played in them.

103
00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:19.995
Yes. His lawyer took great
pains to bring that out.

104
00:06:19.996 --> 00:06:22.623
He had three months from the night
of the murder to the day of the trial...

105
00:06:22.624 --> 00:06:24.333
in which to memorize them.

106
00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:27.086
It's not difficult for a lawyer to find out
what played at a particular theater...

107
00:06:27.087 --> 00:06:28.838
on a particular night.

108
00:06:28.839 --> 00:06:30.654
I'll take the testimony
of the policemen who

109
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.550
interrogated the boy right
after the murder...

110
00:06:32.551 --> 00:06:35.052
when he couldn't remember
a thing about the movies...

111
00:06:35.053 --> 00:06:37.054
great emotional stress or not.

112
00:06:37.055 --> 00:06:40.432
- I'd like to ask you a personal question.
- Go ahead.

113
00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:43.769
- Where were you last night?
- I was home.

114
00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.397
- How about the night before that?
- Come on! What is this?

115
00:06:46.398 --> 00:06:47.815
No, it's all right.

116
00:06:47.816 --> 00:06:50.442
I left the office at 8:30 and
went straight home and to bed.

117
00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:52.194
And the night before that?

118
00:06:52.195 --> 00:06:54.697
That was, uh, Tuesday night?

119
00:06:54.698 --> 00:06:56.782
The night of the bridge tournament.
I played bridge.

120
00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:58.200
Monday night?

121
00:06:58.201 --> 00:07:01.512
When you get down to New
Year's Eve, 1954, let me know, huh?

122
00:07:03.415 --> 00:07:05.474
Monday night?

123
00:07:06.626 --> 00:07:09.712
Monday night, uh, my wife
and I went to the movies.

124
00:07:09.713 --> 00:07:11.422
- What did you see?
- The Scarlet Circle.

125
00:07:11.423 --> 00:07:14.758
- It was a clever whodunit.
- What was the second feature?

126
00:07:14.759 --> 00:07:17.469
The, um...

127
00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.222
I'll tell you in a minute.
The, uh...

128
00:07:20.223 --> 00:07:24.476
Remarkable Mrs., uh, something...

129
00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:27.021
The...

130
00:07:27.022 --> 00:07:29.773
Mrs. Bainbridge.
The Remarkable Mrs. Bainbridge.

131
00:07:29.774 --> 00:07:32.626
I saw that. It's called
The Amazing Mrs. Bainbridge.

132
00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:36.280
Uh, yes. The Amazing Mrs. Bainbridge.
I think that's right.

133
00:07:36.281 --> 00:07:38.866
Who was in The Amazing
Mrs. Bainbridge?

134
00:07:38.867 --> 00:07:42.620
Barbara... Long, I think it was.

135
00:07:42.621 --> 00:07:44.722
A dark, very pretty girl.

136
00:07:45.957 --> 00:07:48.542
Ling or Long...
something like that.

137
00:07:48.543 --> 00:07:51.837
- Who else?
- I'd never heard of them before.

138
00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:56.342
It was a very inexpensive second
feature with, uh, unknown...

139
00:07:56.343 --> 00:07:59.570
And you weren't under an
emotional stress, were you?

140
00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.600
No.

141
00:08:04.601 --> 00:08:06.368
I wasn't.

142
00:08:08.146 --> 00:08:09.980
I think the point is made.

143
00:08:09.981 --> 00:08:12.816
Big point!

144
00:08:12.817 --> 00:08:15.069
You can talk till your tongue
is draggin' on the floor.

145
00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:19.240
The boy is guilty, period.
You know what I mean, my friend?

146
00:08:19.241 --> 00:08:22.593
- Who's got those cough drops?
- They're all gone, my friend.

147
00:08:24.496 --> 00:08:26.830
Oh, boy. Look at that rain.

148
00:08:26.831 --> 00:08:29.291
There goes your ball game.

149
00:08:29.292 --> 00:08:31.794
It's only a shower.

150
00:08:31.795 --> 00:08:34.046
Besides, they got the
infield covered.

151
00:08:34.047 --> 00:08:37.316
Right. Say, could I see that
knife a second, please?

152
00:08:40.011 --> 00:08:42.429
Well, we're still
tied up six to six.

153
00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:44.515
Who's got a suggestion?

154
00:08:44.516 --> 00:08:46.600
It's five after 6:00.
Let's get some dinner.

155
00:08:46.601 --> 00:08:49.144
Why don't we wait till 7:00,
give it another hour, huh?

156
00:08:49.145 --> 00:08:51.355
Okay with me.

157
00:08:51.356 --> 00:08:53.607
Uh, there's something
I'd like to say.

158
00:08:53.608 --> 00:08:57.236
I mean, it's been bothering me a little,
and as long as we're stuck.

159
00:08:57.237 --> 00:09:01.198
Well, there was this whole business about
the stab wound and how it was made...

160
00:09:01.199 --> 00:09:02.908
the downward angle of it, you know?

161
00:09:02.909 --> 00:09:04.994
Don't tell me we're gonna
start with that again.

162
00:09:04.995 --> 00:09:06.704
They've been over it and over it.

163
00:09:06.705 --> 00:09:09.081
Well, I know they did,
but I don't go along with it.

164
00:09:09.082 --> 00:09:13.168
Now, the boy was 5 feet 7
inches tall. His father was 6'2...

165
00:09:13.169 --> 00:09:15.421
That's a difference
of seven inches.

166
00:09:15.422 --> 00:09:18.173
It's a very awkward thing to stab
down into the chest of someone...

167
00:09:18.174 --> 00:09:20.259
who's more than half a
foot taller than you are.

168
00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.469
Give me that.

169
00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:25.364
I'll give you a demonstration.
Somebody get up.

170
00:09:26.641 --> 00:09:28.659
You...

171
00:09:33.064 --> 00:09:35.858
I want you to watch this 'cause
I don't want to have to do it again.

172
00:09:35.859 --> 00:09:38.694
I'll make myself about six or
seven inches shorter, okay?

173
00:09:38.695 --> 00:09:41.922
- It's about right. Maybe a little more.
- Okay. A little more.

174
00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:52.141
That's not funny.

175
00:09:54.127 --> 00:09:58.063
Now, nobody's hurt. Right?

176
00:09:58.631 --> 00:10:00.524
Right. Nobody hurt.

177
00:10:03.887 --> 00:10:08.766
Now, this is the way I'd stab a
man who was taller than I was.

178
00:10:08.767 --> 00:10:11.310
Look at the angle... down and in.

179
00:10:11.311 --> 00:10:13.479
And this is the way it was done.

180
00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.747
Now tell me I'm wrong.

181
00:10:17.859 --> 00:10:21.362
- Down and in. I guess there's no argument.
- Hold it a minute, will ya?

182
00:10:21.363 --> 00:10:23.297
Can you give me that?

183
00:10:24.199 --> 00:10:26.200
Boy, I hate these things.

184
00:10:26.201 --> 00:10:27.951
- Did you ever see a knife fight?
- No.

185
00:10:27.952 --> 00:10:29.620
- You?
- No.

186
00:10:29.621 --> 00:10:32.498
Anybody here ever
see a knife fight?

187
00:10:32.499 --> 00:10:35.376
Well, I have. You know,
on my back stoop...

188
00:10:35.377 --> 00:10:37.795
the lot across
the street, backyard.

189
00:10:37.796 --> 00:10:40.297
Switchblades came with the
neighborhood where I lived.

190
00:10:40.298 --> 00:10:43.634
It's funny I never thought of it before.
I guess you try to forget those things.

191
00:10:43.635 --> 00:10:47.112
- How do you use a switchblade?
- Well, you'd never use it like this.

192
00:10:48.765 --> 00:10:51.158
See, you use too much
time switching hands.

193
00:10:52.811 --> 00:10:55.412
Here's how... underhanded.

194
00:10:58.024 --> 00:11:01.151
Anyone who's ever used a switch knife
wouldn't handle it any other way.

195
00:11:01.152 --> 00:11:03.862
- Are you sure?
- I'm sure.

196
00:11:03.863 --> 00:11:05.781
That's why they're made
to open like that.

197
00:11:05.782 --> 00:11:07.950
You'd say the boy was
pretty handy with a knife?

198
00:11:07.976 --> 00:11:08.492
Mm-hmm.

199
00:11:08.493 --> 00:11:11.745
You think he could have made the kind
of wound that killed his father?

200
00:11:11.746 --> 00:11:15.749
No. Not with the experience he'd had
all his life handling these things.

201
00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:17.835
I feel he'd have gone
for him underhanded.

202
00:11:17.836 --> 00:11:20.963
How do you know? Were you in the
room when the father was killed?

203
00:11:20.964 --> 00:11:23.048
No. Neither was anybody else.

204
00:11:23.049 --> 00:11:26.343
So what are you giving us all this
mumbo jumbo for? I don't believe it.

205
00:11:26.344 --> 00:11:27.994
I don't think you can
determine what type of

206
00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:29.680
wound the boy might or
might not have made...

207
00:11:29.681 --> 00:11:32.391
simply because he knew
how to handle a knife.

208
00:11:32.392 --> 00:11:34.810
What do you think?

209
00:11:34.811 --> 00:11:36.979
- I don't know.
- What do you mean you don't know?

210
00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:38.914
I don't know.

211
00:11:43.027 --> 00:11:45.112
How about you?

212
00:11:45.113 --> 00:11:47.024
I don't know about the rest
of them, but I'm getting a

213
00:11:47.025 --> 00:11:48.949
little tired of this
yackety-yackin' back and forth.

214
00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:52.494
It's gettin' us nowhere. So I
guess I'll have to break it up.

215
00:11:52.495 --> 00:11:55.122
I change my vote to not guilty.

216
00:11:55.123 --> 00:11:58.542
- You what?
- You heard me. I've had enough.

217
00:11:58.543 --> 00:12:01.086
What do you mean you've had enough?
That's no answer!

218
00:12:01.087 --> 00:12:05.132
Hey, listen. You just take care
of yourself, huh? You know?

219
00:12:05.133 --> 00:12:09.178
He's right. That's not an answer.

220
00:12:09.179 --> 00:12:12.222
What kind of a man are you?

221
00:12:12.223 --> 00:12:15.434
You have sat here and voted
guilty with everyone else...

222
00:12:15.435 --> 00:12:19.771
because there are some baseball tickets
burning a hole in your pocket.

223
00:12:19.772 --> 00:12:24.126
And now you've changed your vote because
you say you're sick of all the talking here?

224
00:12:25.028 --> 00:12:26.570
Now listen, buddy!

225
00:12:26.571 --> 00:12:31.325
Who tells you that you have the right
to play like this with a man's life?

226
00:12:31.326 --> 00:12:33.911
- Don't you care...
- Now wait a minute!

227
00:12:33.912 --> 00:12:37.998
- You can't talk like that to me!
- I can talk like that to you.

228
00:12:37.999 --> 00:12:39.958
If you want to vote not guilty...

229
00:12:39.959 --> 00:12:42.419
then do it because you are
convinced the man is not guilty...

230
00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:44.296
not because you've had enough.

231
00:12:44.297 --> 00:12:46.965
And if you think he is guilty,
then vote that way.

232
00:12:46.966 --> 00:12:50.260
Or don't you have the guts to
do what you think is right?

233
00:12:50.261 --> 00:12:52.613
- Now listen.
- Guilty or not guilty?

234
00:12:53.306 --> 00:12:56.992
- I told you, not guilty.
- Why?

235
00:12:58.561 --> 00:13:00.270
Look, I don't have to...

236
00:13:00.271 --> 00:13:03.482
You do have to! Say it! Why?

237
00:13:03.483 --> 00:13:06.043
All right. I don't, uh,
think he's guilty.

238
00:13:15.453 --> 00:13:17.162
I want another vote.

239
00:13:17.163 --> 00:13:19.748
Okay. There's another
vote called for.

240
00:13:19.749 --> 00:13:22.709
I guess the quickest way is a
show of hands. Anyone object?

241
00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.729
Okay. All those voting not
guilty, raise your hands.

242
00:13:26.548 --> 00:13:31.818
One, two, three, four,
five, six, seven...

243
00:13:34.556 --> 00:13:36.323
eight.

244
00:13:40.144 --> 00:13:42.371
Um, nine.

245
00:13:43.273 --> 00:13:48.360
Um, all those voting guilty,
raise your hands.

246
00:13:48.361 --> 00:13:50.821
One, two, three.

247
00:13:50.822 --> 00:13:53.423
Well, the vote's nine to
three in favor of acquittal.

248
00:13:54.325 --> 00:13:56.577
I don't understand you people!

249
00:13:56.578 --> 00:13:58.829
I mean, all these picky little
points you keep bringing up.

250
00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:02.249
They don't mean nothing!
You saw this kid just like I did.

251
00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:05.836
You're not gonna tell me you believe that
phony story about losing the knife...

252
00:14:05.837 --> 00:14:07.921
and that business about
being at the movies.

253
00:14:07.922 --> 00:14:11.008
Look, you know how these
people lie. It's born in them.

254
00:14:11.009 --> 00:14:13.135
I mean, what the heck.
I don't have to tell you.

255
00:14:13.136 --> 00:14:15.178
They don't know what the truth is.

256
00:14:15.179 --> 00:14:18.974
And let me tell you, they don't need any
real big reason to kill someone either.

257
00:14:18.975 --> 00:14:20.809
No, sir.

258
00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:23.937
They get drunk! Oh, they're
real big drinkers, all of 'em.

259
00:14:23.938 --> 00:14:27.190
You know that. And bang...
someone's lying in the gutter.

260
00:14:27.191 --> 00:14:30.277
Well, nobody's blaming them for it.
That's the way they are, by nature.

261
00:14:30.278 --> 00:14:32.446
You know what I mean? Violent!

262
00:14:32.447 --> 00:14:34.031
Where are you going?

263
00:14:34.032 --> 00:14:37.134
Human life don't mean as much
to them as it does to us.

264
00:14:38.036 --> 00:14:41.121
Look! They're lushing it up
and fighting all the time...

265
00:14:41.122 --> 00:14:43.206
and if somebody gets killed,
so somebody gets killed!

266
00:14:43.207 --> 00:14:44.666
They don't care!

267
00:14:44.667 --> 00:14:46.960
Oh, sure, there's some
good things about 'em too.

268
00:14:46.961 --> 00:14:49.004
Look, I'm the first
one to say that.

269
00:14:49.005 --> 00:14:52.382
I've known a couple who were okay, but
that's the exception, you know what I mean?

270
00:14:52.383 --> 00:14:56.219
Most of 'em, it's like they have
no feelings. They can do anything.

271
00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.889
What's going on here?

272
00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:01.016
I'm... I'm trying to tell ya...

273
00:15:01.017 --> 00:15:03.101
you're making a big
mistake, you people.

274
00:15:03.102 --> 00:15:06.204
This kid is a liar! I know it.
I know all about them.

275
00:15:07.815 --> 00:15:10.067
Listen to me. They're no good.

276
00:15:10.068 --> 00:15:12.502
There's not a one of
'em who's any good.

277
00:15:14.447 --> 00:15:16.990
I mean, what...
What's happening in here?

278
00:15:16.991 --> 00:15:19.342
I'm speaking my piece, and you...

279
00:15:20.453 --> 00:15:22.220
Listen to me.

280
00:15:23.206 --> 00:15:24.748
I...

281
00:15:24.749 --> 00:15:27.292
We're... This kid on
trial here, his type...

282
00:15:27.293 --> 00:15:29.394
Well, don't you know about them?

283
00:15:31.255 --> 00:15:34.800
Th-There's...
There's a danger here.

284
00:15:34.801 --> 00:15:36.968
These people are dangerous.

285
00:15:36.969 --> 00:15:40.097
They're... wild.

286
00:15:40.098 --> 00:15:42.099
Listen to me.

287
00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:44.518
- Listen.
- I have.

288
00:15:44.519 --> 00:15:47.621
Now sit down and don't
open your mouth again.

289
00:15:51.442 --> 00:15:54.294
I was only trying to... tell you.

290
00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:35.085
It's always difficult to keep personal
prejudice out of a thing like this.

291
00:16:36.112 --> 00:16:40.215
Wherever you run into it,
prejudice always obscures the truth.

292
00:16:41.701 --> 00:16:44.161
I don't really know
what the truth is.

293
00:16:44.162 --> 00:16:47.180
I don't suppose anybody
will ever really know.

294
00:16:48.374 --> 00:16:52.419
Nine of us now seem to feel
that the defendant is innocent.

295
00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:55.505
But we're just gambling
on probabilities.

296
00:16:55.506 --> 00:16:57.549
We may be wrong.

297
00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:00.010
We may be trying to let
a guilty man go free.

298
00:17:00.011 --> 00:17:01.553
I don't know.

299
00:17:01.554 --> 00:17:03.638
Nobody really can.

300
00:17:03.639 --> 00:17:06.099
But we have a reasonable doubt...

301
00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:09.686
and that's something that's
very valuable in our system.

302
00:17:09.687 --> 00:17:14.332
No jury can declare a man
guilty unless it's sure.

303
00:17:15.693 --> 00:17:18.153
We nine can't understand...

304
00:17:18.154 --> 00:17:20.964
how you three are still so sure.

305
00:17:21.699 --> 00:17:23.800
- Maybe you can tell us.
- I'll try.

306
00:17:25.077 --> 00:17:28.663
You've made some excellent points, but I
still believe the boy is guilty of murder...

307
00:17:28.664 --> 00:17:30.457
and I have two reasons.

308
00:17:30.458 --> 00:17:33.668
One, the evidence given by the
woman across the street...

309
00:17:33.669 --> 00:17:36.379
who actually saw the
murder committed.

310
00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:39.049
As far as I'm concerned,
that's the most important testimony.

311
00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:42.385
And two, the fact that she described
the stabbing by saying...

312
00:17:42.386 --> 00:17:48.099
she saw the boy raise his arm over his head
and stab down into the father's chest.

313
00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:50.227
She saw him do it the wrong way.

314
00:17:50.228 --> 00:17:52.229
That's absolutely right.

315
00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.898
Let's talk about this
woman for a moment.

316
00:17:54.899 --> 00:17:58.068
She said she went to bed
about 11:00 that night.

317
00:17:58.069 --> 00:18:01.738
Her bed was next to the window,
and she could look out while lying down...

318
00:18:01.739 --> 00:18:05.909
and see directly into the
boy's room across the street.

319
00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.620
She tossed and turned for over
an hour, unable to sleep.

320
00:18:08.621 --> 00:18:12.874
Finally, she turned toward the window
at about 10 minutes after 12:00...

321
00:18:12.875 --> 00:18:18.088
and, as she looked out, she saw the killing
through the windows of a passing el train.

322
00:18:18.089 --> 00:18:20.882
She said the lights went out
immediately after the killing...

323
00:18:20.883 --> 00:18:25.779
but that she got a good look at the boy
in the act of stabbing his father.

324
00:18:26.681 --> 00:18:30.617
As far as I can see it,
this is unshakable testimony.

325
00:18:31.269 --> 00:18:34.037
- Well, that's the whole case.
- What do you think?

326
00:18:39.318 --> 00:18:41.086
How about you?

327
00:18:42.613 --> 00:18:45.156
I-I don't know.

328
00:18:45.157 --> 00:18:47.242
So much evidence to sift.

329
00:18:47.243 --> 00:18:49.286
This is a pretty
complicated business.

330
00:18:49.287 --> 00:18:53.164
Frankly, I don't see how you
can vote for acquittal.

331
00:18:53.165 --> 00:18:55.584
It's not so easy to arrange
all the evidence in order.

332
00:18:55.585 --> 00:18:57.711
You can throw out
all the other evidence!

333
00:18:57.712 --> 00:19:01.381
The woman saw him do it!
What else do you want?

334
00:19:01.382 --> 00:19:03.341
- Yeah, well, maybe...
- Let's vote on it.

335
00:19:03.342 --> 00:19:05.594
Okay. There's another
vote called for.

336
00:19:05.595 --> 00:19:07.512
Anybody object?

337
00:19:07.513 --> 00:19:10.640
All right. I'm changing my vote.
He's guilty.

338
00:19:10.641 --> 00:19:13.143
Anybody else?
The vote is eight to four.

339
00:19:13.144 --> 00:19:16.813
Why is this such a personal
triumph for you... this one vote?

340
00:19:16.814 --> 00:19:20.358
Okay. I say we're a hung jury.
I say we take it in to the judge.

341
00:19:20.359 --> 00:19:23.153
How about it?
I want to hear arguments.

342
00:19:23.154 --> 00:19:25.755
You, you're the leader of
the cause. What about it?

343
00:19:27.909 --> 00:19:29.826
Let's go over it again.

344
00:19:29.827 --> 00:19:31.828
We've been over it again!

345
00:19:31.829 --> 00:19:34.407
The boy in the gray flannel
suit here is bouncin'

346
00:19:34.408 --> 00:19:36.708
backwards and forwards
like a tennis ball.

347
00:19:36.709 --> 00:19:40.645
No point in getting nasty and
trying to turn this into a contest.

348
00:19:42.506 --> 00:19:44.049
Okay.

349
00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:47.177
Maybe we can talk about setting
some kind of time limit.

350
00:19:47.178 --> 00:19:49.404
Yeah, once around
for the deal, huh?

351
00:19:50.014 --> 00:19:53.850
It's a quarter after 6:00.

352
00:19:53.851 --> 00:19:55.894
Someone before mentioned 7:00.

353
00:19:55.895 --> 00:19:58.355
I think that's a point at which
we might begin to discuss...

354
00:19:58.356 --> 00:20:01.691
the question of whether
we're a hung jury or not.

355
00:20:01.692 --> 00:20:03.460
Don't you feel well?

356
00:20:04.695 --> 00:20:06.738
I feel perfectly well, thank you.

357
00:20:06.739 --> 00:20:09.532
I was saying that 7:00
would be a reasonable time.

358
00:20:09.533 --> 00:20:12.535
Uh, the reason I asked about
that was because, uh...

359
00:20:12.536 --> 00:20:14.204
you were rubbing your nose like...

360
00:20:14.205 --> 00:20:16.957
Oh, I'm sorry for interrupting...

361
00:20:16.958 --> 00:20:20.251
but you made a gesture that
reminded me of something.

362
00:20:20.252 --> 00:20:22.379
I'm trying to settle something
here. Do you mind?

363
00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.481
Well, I think this is important.

364
00:20:25.466 --> 00:20:27.342
Thank you.

365
00:20:27.343 --> 00:20:30.053
Now then, I'm sure you'll
pardon me for this...

366
00:20:30.054 --> 00:20:33.056
but I was wondering why you were
rubbing your nose like that.

367
00:20:33.057 --> 00:20:34.808
Oh, come on, will ya?

368
00:20:34.809 --> 00:20:39.062
At this point, I happen to be talking
to the gentleman sitting next to you!

369
00:20:39.063 --> 00:20:42.399
Now, why were you rubbing
your nose like that?

370
00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:46.569
Well, if it's any of your business, I was
rubbing it because it bothers me a little.

371
00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:48.279
Oh. I'm sorry.

372
00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:52.242
- Is it... Is it because of your eyeglasses?
- It is.

373
00:20:52.243 --> 00:20:54.077
Now can we get on
to something else?

374
00:20:54.078 --> 00:20:58.748
Your eyeglasses made those two deep
impressions on the sides of your nose.

375
00:20:58.749 --> 00:21:01.001
I hadn't noticed that before.

376
00:21:01.002 --> 00:21:04.587
- That must be annoying.
- It is very annoying.

377
00:21:04.588 --> 00:21:06.589
Well, I wouldn't know about that.

378
00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:10.010
I've never worn eyeglasses.
Twenty-twenty.

379
00:21:10.011 --> 00:21:12.654
Listen, will you come on already
with the optometrist bit?

380
00:21:14.473 --> 00:21:18.101
The woman who testified
that she saw the killing...

381
00:21:18.102 --> 00:21:21.746
had those same marks on
the sides of her nose.

382
00:21:22.648 --> 00:21:24.941
Holy smokes! You know, he's right.

383
00:21:24.942 --> 00:21:26.693
Please.

384
00:21:26.694 --> 00:21:30.530
Just give me a minute,
and I'll be finished.

385
00:21:30.531 --> 00:21:33.450
I don't know if anyone else
noticed that about her.

386
00:21:33.451 --> 00:21:37.428
I didn't think of it then,
but I've been going over her face...

387
00:21:38.372 --> 00:21:40.582
in my mind.

388
00:21:40.583 --> 00:21:42.684
She had those same marks.

389
00:21:43.586 --> 00:21:47.856
- She kept rubbing them in court.
- He's right. She did do that a lot.

390
00:21:48.799 --> 00:21:53.553
This woman was about, uh...
about 45 years old.

391
00:21:53.554 --> 00:21:57.599
She was making a tremendous
effort to look 35...

392
00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:59.893
for her first public appearance.

393
00:21:59.894 --> 00:22:03.229
Heavy makeup, dyed hair...

394
00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:07.192
brand-new clothes that should have
been worn by a younger woman.

395
00:22:07.193 --> 00:22:08.943
No glasses.

396
00:22:08.944 --> 00:22:10.695
No, women do that.

397
00:22:10.696 --> 00:22:13.198
See if you can get the
mental picture of it.

398
00:22:13.199 --> 00:22:15.116
What do you mean, no glasses?

399
00:22:15.117 --> 00:22:17.911
How do you know whether she wore glasses?
Just 'cause she was rubbing her nose?

400
00:22:17.912 --> 00:22:20.205
Now, she had those
marks. I saw 'em.

401
00:22:20.206 --> 00:22:22.207
So what? What do ya
think that means?

402
00:22:22.208 --> 00:22:24.475
Listen, I'm getting so sick
of your yelling in here!

403
00:22:24.501 --> 00:22:25.502
Come on. Forget it.

404
00:22:25.503 --> 00:22:28.588
Hey, listen. Listen, he's right.
I saw them too.

405
00:22:28.589 --> 00:22:31.800
I was the closest one to her. She had these,
these things on the side of her nose.

406
00:22:31.801 --> 00:22:35.095
- What do you call those on the side?
- Well, what point are you making here?

407
00:22:35.096 --> 00:22:39.057
She had dyed hair, marks on her nose.
Well, what does that mean?

408
00:22:39.058 --> 00:22:43.453
Could those marks be made by
anything other than eyeglasses?

409
00:22:46.941 --> 00:22:49.359
No, they couldn't.

410
00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:51.461
I didn't see any marks!

411
00:22:52.988 --> 00:22:54.781
I did.

412
00:22:54.782 --> 00:22:57.408
Strange, but I didn't
think about it before.

413
00:22:57.409 --> 00:22:59.619
Well, what about the lawyer?
Why didn't he say something?

414
00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:02.622
There are 12 people in here
concentrating on this case.

415
00:23:02.623 --> 00:23:04.791
Eleven of us didn't
think of it either.

416
00:23:04.792 --> 00:23:08.044
Well, what about the district attorney?
Do you think he'd pull a trick like that?

417
00:23:08.045 --> 00:23:10.021
Have her testify
without her glasses?

418
00:23:10.047 --> 00:23:11.953
Did you ever see a woman who
had to wear glasses and

419
00:23:11.954 --> 00:23:14.050
didn't want to because she
thinks they spoil her looks?

420
00:23:14.051 --> 00:23:17.637
Okay. She had marks on her nose.

421
00:23:17.638 --> 00:23:21.391
I'm giving you that.
From glasses, right?

422
00:23:21.392 --> 00:23:25.645
She didn't want to wear 'em out of the house
so people would think she's gorgeous.

423
00:23:25.646 --> 00:23:30.316
But when she saw this kid killing his
father, she was in the house alone.

424
00:23:30.317 --> 00:23:32.610
That's all.

425
00:23:32.611 --> 00:23:36.714
- Do you wear glasses when you go to bed?
- No, I don't.

426
00:23:38.325 --> 00:23:40.326
No one wears eyeglasses to bed.

427
00:23:40.327 --> 00:23:43.413
It's logical to assume that she wasn't
wearing them when she was in bed...

428
00:23:43.414 --> 00:23:45.248
tossing and turning,
trying to fall asleep.

429
00:23:45.249 --> 00:23:47.667
- How do you know?
- I don't know! I'm guessing.

430
00:23:47.668 --> 00:23:50.211
I'm also guessing that she probably
didn't put her glasses on...

431
00:23:50.212 --> 00:23:52.422
when she turned to look
casually out of the window.

432
00:23:52.423 --> 00:23:55.258
And she herself testified the killing
took place just as she looked out.

433
00:23:55.259 --> 00:23:56.718
The lights went off a
split second later.

434
00:23:56.744 --> 00:23:58.386
She couldn't have had
time to put them on then.

435
00:23:58.387 --> 00:24:00.096
- Wait a second.
- Here's another guess.

436
00:24:00.097 --> 00:24:04.267
Maybe she honestly thought she saw the boy
kill his father. I say she only saw a blur.

437
00:24:04.268 --> 00:24:07.937
How do you know what she saw?
How does he know all that?

438
00:24:07.938 --> 00:24:10.773
How do you know what kind of glasses
she wore? Maybe they were sunglasses.

439
00:24:10.774 --> 00:24:13.484
Maybe she was farsighted.
What do you know about it?

440
00:24:13.485 --> 00:24:16.821
I only know the woman's
eyesight is in question now.

441
00:24:16.822 --> 00:24:23.036
She had to be able to identify a person
60 feet away, at night, without glasses.

442
00:24:23.037 --> 00:24:25.496
You can't send someone off to
die on evidence like that.

443
00:24:25.497 --> 00:24:27.582
Oh, don't give me that.

444
00:24:27.583 --> 00:24:29.834
Don't you think the woman
might have made a mistake?

445
00:24:29.835 --> 00:24:32.337
- No.
- It's not possible?

446
00:24:32.338 --> 00:24:34.063
No, it's not possible.

447
00:24:36.926 --> 00:24:38.943
Is it possible?

448
00:24:39.970 --> 00:24:41.738
Not guilty.

449
00:24:45.809 --> 00:24:48.561
Do you think he's guilty?

450
00:24:48.562 --> 00:24:50.663
I think he's guilty.

451
00:24:53.317 --> 00:24:55.084
Do you?

452
00:24:57.863 --> 00:25:00.990
No. I'm convinced.

453
00:25:00.991 --> 00:25:02.659
Not guilty.

454
00:25:02.660 --> 00:25:04.535
What's the matter with you?

455
00:25:04.536 --> 00:25:06.537
I have a reasonable doubt now.

456
00:25:06.538 --> 00:25:08.164
Eleven to one.

457
00:25:08.165 --> 00:25:11.167
Well, what about all the other evidence?
What about all that stuff?

458
00:25:11.168 --> 00:25:13.336
The knife, the whole business!

459
00:25:13.337 --> 00:25:16.356
Well, you said we could throw
out all the other evidence.

460
00:25:22.012 --> 00:25:24.280
Well, what do we do now?

461
00:25:27.184 --> 00:25:28.935
You're alone.

462
00:25:28.936 --> 00:25:32.872
I don't care whether
I'm alone or not! It's my right.

463
00:25:35.943 --> 00:25:38.211
It's your right.

464
00:25:47.246 --> 00:25:49.764
Well, what do you want?
I say he's guilty.

465
00:25:51.292 --> 00:25:54.727
- We want to hear your arguments.
- I gave you my arguments.

466
00:25:55.629 --> 00:25:57.839
We're not convinced.

467
00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:00.717
We want to hear them again.

468
00:26:00.718 --> 00:26:02.819
We have as much time as it takes.

469
00:26:08.392 --> 00:26:12.937
Everything... Every single thing
that took place in that courtroom...

470
00:26:12.938 --> 00:26:14.939
but I mean everything...
says he's guilty.

471
00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.500
What do you think,
I'm an idiot or something?

472
00:26:19.069 --> 00:26:21.237
Why don't you take that
stuff about the old man?

473
00:26:21.238 --> 00:26:24.507
The old man who lived there
and heard everything.

474
00:26:26.076 --> 00:26:27.827
Or this business about the knife.

475
00:26:27.828 --> 00:26:29.912
What? 'Cause he found another
one exactly like it?

476
00:26:29.913 --> 00:26:33.333
The old man saw him right
there on the stairs!

477
00:26:33.334 --> 00:26:36.269
What's the difference how
many seconds it was?

478
00:26:37.338 --> 00:26:39.439
Every single thing.

479
00:26:40.758 --> 00:26:44.010
The knife falling through
a hole in his pocket.

480
00:26:44.011 --> 00:26:47.430
You can't prove he
didn't get to the door.

481
00:26:47.431 --> 00:26:49.932
Sure, you can take all the time,
hobble around the room.

482
00:26:49.933 --> 00:26:52.618
But you can't prove it!

483
00:26:54.438 --> 00:26:58.149
And what about this business
of the el and the movies?

484
00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:01.277
There's a phony deal
if I ever heard one.

485
00:27:01.278 --> 00:27:05.573
I'll bet ya $5,000 I'd
remember the movies I saw.

486
00:27:05.574 --> 00:27:07.909
I'm telling ya, everything
that's gone on...

487
00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:11.829
has been twisted and turned!

488
00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:13.998
This business with the glasses?

489
00:27:13.999 --> 00:27:19.629
How do you know she didn't have them on?
This woman testified in open court!

490
00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:23.674
And what about hearing
the kid yell, huh?

491
00:27:23.675 --> 00:27:27.028
I'm telling ya, I've got
all the facts here.

492
00:27:28.097 --> 00:27:30.072
Here. Ah...

493
00:27:32.351 --> 00:27:34.952
Well, that's it!
That's the whole case.

494
00:27:42.069 --> 00:27:43.753
Well?

495
00:27:45.948 --> 00:27:48.049
Say something!

496
00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:56.766
You lousy bunch of bleeding hearts.

497
00:27:57.334 --> 00:27:59.335
You're not gonna intimidate me.

498
00:27:59.336 --> 00:28:01.938
I'm entitled to my opinion.

499
00:28:05.217 --> 00:28:09.612
Rotten kids.
You work your life out!

500
00:28:14.351 --> 00:28:16.410
I can't keep...

501
00:28:20.983 --> 00:28:22.750
No.

502
00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:28.464
Not guilty.

503
00:28:31.743 --> 00:28:33.511
Not guilty.

504
00:28:36.415 --> 00:28:38.124
Not...

505
00:28:48.594 --> 00:28:50.861
Um, we're ready now.

506
00:30:44.418 --> 00:30:46.185
Hey!

507
00:30:50.424 --> 00:30:52.174
What's your name?

508
00:30:52.175 --> 00:30:53.759
Davis.

509
00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.778
My name's McCardle.

510
00:30:58.015 --> 00:30:59.765
Well, so long.

511
00:30:59.766 --> 00:31:01.367
So long.
